INT. AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL TOWER, SMALL REGIONAL AIRPORT - NIGHT

A glass-walled octagon above a dark runway. Radar scope glows green. A cold cup of coffee sits beside a ham sandwich with one bite out of it.

OSWALD "OZZIE" FAIRWEATHER (60s, headset, cardigan) sits at the console, steady and unhurried. Next to him stands his granddaughter, PRUDENCE "PRU" FAIRWEATHER (17, school uniform, backpack), holding a notebook. She is supposed to be doing homework. She is not.

Two others fill the cramped room. One is MARTIN ESPERANZA (30s, shift supervisor, tie loosened), who hovers over the radar. The other is a trainee, JUNE TAKAHASHI (19, apron over uniform, nervous), who clutches a clipboard.

OZZIE
Pru, if you're going to watch, watch properly. Come around here.

PRU
Grandpa, I'm fine.

OZZIE
Come on.

She steps beside him. He nods to the scope.

OZZIE (CONT'D)
That blip is a Cessna. November-Four-Two-Seven. See how she drifts?

PRU
Why?

OZZIE
Crosswind. He's fighting it.

Pru leans closer. Her breath fogs the glass.

PILOT (V.O.)
(over radio, crackling)
Tower, Cessna Four-Two-Seven, ten miles out, requesting landing.

Ozzie squeezes the mic.

OZZIE
(into radio)
Four-Two-Seven, Tower, runway two-seven, wind two-six-zero at eighteen gusting twenty-five. Say your fuel state.

PILOT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Tower, Four-Two-Seven, thirty minutes of fuel. We're good.

MARTIN
(muttering)
Thirty minutes. In that wind.

JUNE
Should I call the fire crew?

MARTIN
Not yet.

Ozzie hands the headset to Pru.

PRU
Grandpa, no.

OZZIE
Just say what I say. "Four-Two-Seven, cleared to land."

She hesitates, then pulls the headset on. Her voice shakes.

PRU
(into radio)
Four-Two-Seven, cleared to land.

PILOT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Cleared to land, Four-Two-Seven. Who's this? You sound young.

PRU
Um. I'm... training.

PILOT (V.O.)
(over radio)
Good job, trainee. See you on the ground.

Pru stares at her grandfather, wide-eyed. He chuckles and pats her shoulder.

OZZIE
Eyes on the scope, Pru. That's the whole job.

Below them, tiny landing lights wobble through the dark, steadying, lowering. They all watch it touch down.
